<p>This is the third and final post in our <strong>Meet the Speakers</strong> series. We&#8217;ve covered <a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/cppblog/pure-virtual-cpp-2026-meet-the-speakers-part-1-build-faster-run-faster/">building and running faster</a> and the <a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/cppblog/pure-virtual-cpp-2026-meet-the-speakers-part-2-the-ai-native-c-developer-workflow/">AI-native developer workflow</a>. We close with a theme about where the language is headed and how it lives alongside its neighbors: <strong>modernizing C++.</strong></p>
<h2>Why this theme matters</h2>
<p>Modernizing C++ isn&#8217;t only about adopting new standard features, it&#8217;s also about C++&#8217;s place in a world where hybrid codebases are increasingly the norm. Nowhere is that clearer than at the C++/Rust boundary, where two systems languages have to coexist without giving up any performance. <strong>If your codebase is heading toward a mixed future, or is already there, this is the map you need.</strong></p>
